      "problem": "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(2x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$",
